<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stuart Munro-Hay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1947">1947</a>–<a href="/years/2004">2004</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stuart Munro-Hay was a British historian and archaeologist who specialised in the history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Accounts of the Aksumite Empire quote his description of the sixth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> campaign as Aksum's swan song as a great power. He argued it overextended the empire.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Historians such as <b>Stuart Munro-Hay</b> have described this venture as Aksum's swan song as a great power, since it overextended the empire's military and financial resources: within a few years the Aksumite general Abraha had made himself effectively independent ruler of Yemen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Stuart Munro-Hay</b> points to Aksumite influence: Biete Amanuel imitates in stone the wooden features still seen in ancient churches of Tigray and Eritrea, and the framed doors and windows seem to echo the stelae of Aksum.</blockquote><a href="/work/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">Rock-hewn churches of Lalibela · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Stuart Munro-Hay</b> thought that in the early kingdom a new king of kings may have been chosen from among all the kings of a confederacy rather than by birth order.</blockquote><a href="/societies/aksum">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Aksum</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Northern Ireland. Died in Chiang Mai, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.79,98.98&amp;year=1984" aria-label="Chiang Mai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7229 1467 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Stuart Munro-Hay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7679 1737h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7679 1737h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=18.79,98.98&amp;year=1984">What was here in 1984? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
